const articleHtml = "<p>Individual testing asks a question about one bird. Flock screening asks a question about a group. Both are useful, but they answer different questions and carry different limits.</p>\n<p>The choice should follow the decision you need to make, not the size of the laboratory menu.</p>\n<h2>What Is Individual Bird Testing?</h2>\n<p>Individual testing collects and tests a sample from a specific bird. The result is tied to that bird's identity.</p>\n<p>It is the right approach when:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>the bird is clinically abnormal;</li>\n<li>the bird is high-value breeding stock;</li>\n<li>the bird is about to enter a collection;</li>\n<li>a previous group result needs follow-up;</li>\n<li>a veterinarian needs information about a specific patient.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Individual testing is more expensive per bird, but it provides the clearest attribution.</p>\n<h2>What Is Flock Screening?</h2>\n<p>Flock screening samples a group to estimate whether a pathogen is present or circulating. It may involve:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>testing a representative number of birds;</li>\n<li>testing selected high-risk birds;</li>\n<li>pooling samples from several birds;</li>\n<li>repeated sampling over time.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>A flock result is a group-level signal. It does not automatically tell you which bird is positive, and a negative flock result does not prove every bird is negative.</p>\n<h2>When Should Individual Birds Be Tested?</h2>\n<p>Individual testing is most defensible when the consequence of a wrong group-level assumption is high.</p>\n<p>Examples include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>a breeding pair before mating;</li>\n<li>a new bird before introduction;</li>\n<li>a bird with respiratory, neurological, or gastrointestinal signs;</li>\n<li>a bird returning from a show or race;</li>\n<li>follow-up after a positive pool.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The more identifiable the bird and the more valuable the decision, the stronger the case for individual testing.</p>\n<h2>When Is Flock Screening Useful?</h2>\n<p>Flock screening is useful for:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>establishing a baseline in a large collection;</li>\n<li>monitoring trends over time;</li>\n<li>detecting a signal when individual testing is impractical;</li>\n<li>screening a group before a management change;</li>\n<li>investigating a facility-level problem.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>It is not a substitute for clinical examination. A flock can be \"negative\" on a panel and still contain an individual bird with a problem that the sample missed.</p>\n<h2>Individual Sampling vs Pooled Sampling</h2>\n<p>Pooling combines samples from several birds into one test. It reduces laboratory cost and can increase throughput.</p>\n<p>But pooling changes the mathematics of detection. A positive result may come from one bird or several. A negative result may occur because the positive bird's contribution was diluted below the assay's detection limit.</p>\n<p>Pool size, sample volume, target prevalence, and assay sensitivity all matter. A pool that is too large can hide a low-prevalence infection.</p>\n<h2>How Pooling Affects Detection</h2>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Design</th>\n<th>Advantage</th>\n<th>Risk</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Individual samples</td>\n<td>Clear attribution</td>\n<td>Higher cost and handling</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Small pools</td>\n<td>Efficient use of laboratory capacity</td>\n<td>Some dilution; follow-up still needed</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Large pools</td>\n<td>Lower cost per bird</td>\n<td>Higher chance of missing a low-level positive</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Repeated group sampling</td>\n<td>Trend detection</td>\n<td>Does not identify individual status</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>A positive pool should trigger a follow-up plan. Testing every bird may not be necessary; testing the most likely contributors, or using a smaller pool, may be enough.</p>\n<h2>How to Select Representative Birds</h2>\n<p>Representative sampling is not \"pick a few birds that look normal.\" It should reflect the group's risk.</p>\n<p>Consider:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>age groups;</li>\n<li>new arrivals;</li>\n<li>birds with previous positive results;</li>\n<li>birds in high-contact areas;</li>\n<li>clinically abnormal birds;</li>\n<li>birds that have been transported or mixed.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>A convenience sample can produce a falsely reassuring result. The sampling plan should be written before collection.</p>\n<h2>Positive Pool Results and Follow-Up</h2>\n<p>When a pool is positive, the next question is attribution.</p>\n<p>Options include:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>retest individuals from the pool;</li>\n<li>divide the pool into smaller groups;</li>\n<li>prioritise birds with clinical signs or high exposure;</li>\n<li>review the sample type and timing before assuming a true cluster.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>Do not treat a positive pool as a diagnosis for every bird in the group. Do not treat a negative pool as proof that the group is clear.</p>\n<h2>Cost, Accuracy and Operational Considerations</h2>\n<p>Individual testing generally costs more per bird but gives the clearest answer. Pooling can reduce cost, but it can also reduce sensitivity and complicate follow-up.</p>\n<p>The cheapest test is not the one with the lowest invoice. It is the one that answers the question without creating a second round of uncertainty.</p>\n<h2>Choosing a Screening Strategy</h2>\n<p>Use this sequence:</p>\n<p><strong>Decision → required confidence → group size → prevalence assumption → sample type → individual or pooled → follow-up plan.</strong></p>\n<p>A high-value pre-introduction decision may justify individual testing. A large facility's routine monitoring may justify representative or pooled sampling, provided the limitations are understood.</p>\n<h2>Scenario: A 200-bird aviary needs a shipment clearance</h2>\n<p>A 200-bird budgerigar aviary has a shipment deadline and wants pooled samples to tell the buyer the flock is clear. Birds occupy several rooms and include a mix of ages. The buyer has not said whether acceptance needs individual evidence or a group screen, and pool size and volume are unconfirmed. A pool can show target nucleic acid in a group sample, but it cannot attribute a positive to a bird or certify every bird as negative.</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Clarify the shipment decision, the buyer's wording, and whether any part is regulatory. A laboratory result cannot substitute for an official movement requirement.</li>\n<li>Divide birds by room, species, age, and contact group, and pool only within compatible groups. Do not combine samples to reduce the count.</li>\n<li>With the veterinarian, choose representative or risk-biased birds, including those with signs or recent contact. Confirm pool size and target-specific limits.</li>\n<li>If a pool is Not Detected, report a group-level finding with the limitation explicit. If Detected, follow up with individual or smaller-pool testing; check target reporting in our <a href=\"/multiplex-pcr-avian-disease-testing/\">multiplex PCR guide</a>.</li>\n<li>Use individual testing when the decision requires bird-level attribution, signs are present, or a missed positive has serious consequences. Release remains open until the evidence matches the question.</li>\n</ol>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr><th>Result</th><th>What changes</th><th>What it does not prove</th></tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr><td>Pool Not Detected</td><td>Continue planning with the group-level limitation documented.</td><td>Every bird is negative or the shipment is disease-free.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Pool Detected</td><td>Pause release and move to individual or smaller-pool follow-up.</td><td>Which bird carried the target or how far it spread.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Inconclusive or Invalid</td><td>Do not count the result as a clearance; arrange repeat testing as advised.</td><td>Presence or absence of the target.</td></tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p><strong>What we would do:</strong> We would not accept a pooled result as a blanket clearance. We would match the sample design to the shipment decision, because pooling can be efficient for surveillance but cannot identify which bird carried a target. Our <a href=\"/bird-health-screening-biosecurity/\">screening and biosecurity sequence</a> treats this as a question-first process, not a test-first one. This advice would not apply if the buyer or a regulatory programme expressly requires individual results; that requirement would set the sampling plan.</p>\n<p>This is a testing-strategy example, not a diagnosis or treatment plan, and a veterinarian must interpret results with clinical findings.</p>\n<h2>Laboratory Perspective</h2>\n<p>SENO provides avian molecular testing for individual and group samples where the assay and sample type are appropriate. This article is a testing-strategy guide, not a veterinary diagnosis or a guarantee of group freedom from infection. The laboratory should be consulted on pool size, sample volume, and target-specific limits before a pooled batch is planned.</p>\n<h2>Frequently Asked Questions</h2>\n<p><strong>Is individual testing always better?</strong></p>\n<p>No. It gives clearer attribution, but it may be impractical for a large group. The right choice depends on the decision and the consequence of a missed positive.</p>\n<p><strong>Can I pool samples from different species?</strong></p>\n<p>That is usually not advisable without laboratory guidance. Different species, sample types, and target burdens can change the meaning of a pooled result.</p>\n<p><strong>Does a negative pool mean the flock is negative?</strong></p>\n<p>No. It means the target was not detected in that pooled sample under the assay's limits. Individual birds may still be positive.</p>\n<p><strong>What should I do after a positive pool?</strong></p>\n<p>Follow up with individual or smaller-pool testing, prioritising birds with clinical signs or high exposure. Review the sample and timing before assuming a widespread outbreak.</p>\n<p><strong>Can flock screening replace quarantine?</strong></p>\n<p>No. Screening is a detection tool. Quarantine is a physical separation tool. They solve different parts of the biosecurity problem.</p>\n<h2>Sources</h2>\n<ol>\n<li>USDA APHIS — Defend the Flock: Biosecurity. https://www.aphis.usda.gov/livestock-poultry-disease/avian/defend-the-flock</li>\n<li>CDC — Psittacosis: About. https://www.cdc.gov/psittacosis/about/index.html</li>\n<li>Merck Veterinary Manual — Pet birds. https://www.merckvetmanual.com/exotic-and-laboratory-animals/pet-birds</li>\n<li>AAVLD — Accreditation requirements. https://www.aavld.org/accreditation-requirements</li>\n<li>WOAH — Newcastle disease. https://www.woah.org/en/disease/newcastle-disease/</li>\n<li>CDC — Bird flu: About. https://www.cdc.gov/bird-flu/about/index.html</li>\n</ol>";
